= Empirical evidence =

== Overview ==
'''Empirical evidence''' is [[Evidence|evidence]] grounded in observation, experiment, [[Measurement|measurement]], or other experience of the world.

It is central to the empirical sciences because scientific claims about nature are expected to connect in some way to observable consequences, recorded phenomena, instrument outputs, or experimental results.

Empirical evidence is not identical to raw data, and not every empirical observation establishes the interpretation placed on it.

== Observation ==
Observation can be direct, instrument-assisted, or highly processed.

Examples include:
* seeing a visible event;
* recording a temperature;
* sequencing DNA;
* measuring a particle detector signal;
* collecting survey responses;
* observing behavior;
* imaging a distant astronomical object.

Modern science frequently relies on instruments and computational pipelines that extend observation far beyond unaided human senses.

== Experiment ==
An experiment deliberately changes or controls conditions in order to observe consequences.

Experiments can strengthen causal inference because interventions can help distinguish competing explanations. But not every scientific question can be experimentally manipulated.

Astronomy, geology, evolutionary biology, epidemiology, and history of climate often depend heavily on observational evidence.

== Measurement ==
[[Measurement]] converts interactions with a system into structured results under calibration, scale, unit, and model assumptions.

A measurement result can include:
* a measured value;
* a unit;
* uncertainty;
* conditions of measurement;
* calibration/reference information.

Instrument output becomes empirical evidence only after the relation between the instrument, the target, and the result is understood.

== Data and empirical evidence ==
A dataset is a recorded collection of values, observations, events, or representations.

Data become evidence for a claim through:
* relevance to the question;
* measurement validity;
* appropriate analysis;
* background assumptions;
* sampling/design;
* comparison with alternatives.

The same dataset may support different claims or none at all depending on the inferential context.

== Theory-ladenness ==
Observation is not always independent of theory.

Scientists choose:
* what to measure;
* which instrument settings to use;
* how to classify signals;
* what counts as noise;
* which models transform readings into results.

This is sometimes called the '''theory-ladenness of observation'''.

It does not mean observations are merely subjective. Independent instruments, calibration, blind procedures, replication, and cross-method agreement can constrain interpretation.

== Empirical and non-empirical evidence ==
Not all evidence is empirical.

Mathematical proof can establish a theorem without observation. Logical relations can refute an inconsistent argument. Conceptual analysis can clarify implications of definitions.

Scientific inquiry combines empirical evidence with formal reasoning, statistical inference, prior theory, and models.

== Correlation and causation ==
Empirical association is not sufficient by itself for [[Causality|causal inference]].

A causal interpretation may require:
* intervention;
* control of confounding;
* temporal information;
* mechanism;
* structural assumptions;
* robustness across designs.

Observational evidence can support causal claims, but the path from observation to causation must be stated.

== Repetition and convergence ==
Empirical evidence becomes more robust when:
* measurements are repeated;
* independent methods converge;
* different investigators reproduce or [[Replication|replicate]] findings;
* systematic errors are investigated;
* predictions succeed outside the original dataset.

Repeated evidence can still share hidden biases, so independence and [[Provenance|provenance]] matter.

== Epistemic notes ==
"Empirical" does not mean "certain" or "uninterpreted." Empirical evidence is evidence rooted in worldly observation/interaction, but its epistemic strength depends on method, relevance, measurement, and inference.

